{"type":"assistant","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The binary search was using a **left** insertion rule (`sorted_list[mid] < x` → move right). For equal keys that stops at the first match, so new `2`s would go before existing `2`s.\n\nIt now treats `sorted_list[mid] <= x` as “go right,” which is the usual **rightmost** insertion point (same idea as `bisect_right`): equal values are skipped until the run ends, so `insert_index([1, 2, 2, 3], 2)` returns `3`.\n\nAll visible tests pass (`2 passed`)."}]},"session_id":"90fbe334-a5e8-4d8f-96dd-ab331470aedd"}
{"type":"result","subtype":"success","duration_ms":30872,"duration_api_ms":30872,"is_error":false,"result":"Inspecting `insert_index` and running tests.\n\n\nUsing `<=` instead of `<` implements the rightmost insertion point (bisect_right).\n\n\nRemoving the outdated BUG comment.\n\n\nThe binary search was using a **left** insertion rule (`sorted_list[mid] < x` → move right). For equal keys that stops at the first match, so new `2`s would go before existing `2`s.\n\nIt now treats `sorted_list[mid] <= x` as “go right,” which is the usual **rightmost** insertion point (same idea as `bisect_right`): equal values are skipped until the run ends, so `insert_index([1, 2, 2, 3], 2)` returns `3`.\n\nAll visible tests pass (`2 passed`).","session_id":"90fbe334-a5e8-4d8f-96dd-ab331470aedd","request_id":"a9832c02-d69c-49e5-83aa-5b4d063708ed","usage":{"inputTokens":11003,"outputTokens":1169,"cacheReadTokens":47840,"cacheWriteTokens":0}}
